fact WorkItem(id: "WI-414", description: some(value: "BUG: a List op that uses `eq` under List's sort-level `requires Eq[T]` (e.g. `member`, `nth`) TYPECHECKS but FAILS AT RUNTIME when called from an external namespace: `DeferToRequirement: requirement param __req_eq not bound in caller frame`. ROOT: a CONCRETE-typed call inside the op body — `eq(i, 0)` with i:Int, or `eq(head, x)` at a concrete element — routes through the enclosing sort's `requires Eq[T]` (deferred to the __req_eq frame param) instead of resolving concretely to the available `fact Eq[Int]`. EXPECTED (user): it should EITHER fail to COMPILE if Eq genuinely can't be found, OR (the right behavior) resolve `Eq[Int]` concretely so the call works at runtime — NOT compile-clean then crash on a missing requirement. The concrete impl IS present (fact Eq[Int]), so concrete resolution should win. REPRO (both typecheck; run from an OUTSIDE namespace): `member(2, [1,2,3])` -> Err(__req_eq not bound); `length([1,2,3])` -> Ok(3) (length uses `add`/Numeric, no Eq requirement, so it works — isolating the gap to the requires-Eq path). PRE-EXISTING (NOT from the WI-410/413 typing work): demonstrated with `member`, which is unchanged code; the old bare `nth` had the same `eq(i,0)` and the same latent failure. IMPACT: eq-using List ops are not runtime-callable from an external namespace; the requirement-insertion pass (WI-218/231/239) over-defers a requirement whose concrete impl is statically available. FIX DIRECTION: at requirement insertion / dispatch, when a `requires Spec[T]` op is invoked with the relevant position at a CONCRETE type that has a `fact Spec[<concrete>]`, resolve it concretely (pin the impl) instead of threading __req. ACCEPTANCE: `member(2,[1,2,3])` and `nth([10,20,30],0)` eval from an external namespace (no manual requirement binding); full suite stays green."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-06-07T05:56:23Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-414", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED 2026-06-07 (8988de9). The INTERNAL concrete-eq case (nth) — exactly the WI title 'a concrete spec-op call defers instead of resolving the concrete fact'. ROOT: entry_matches_subst (defer-to-requirement matching, typing.rs) treated a CONCRETE per-call value (Eq.T:=Int from eq(i:Int,0) inside a List op) as matching an OPEN-T requirement entry (requires Eq[T], a type-param wildcard) via the symmetric dispatch_values_match — so the call deferred to a __req_eq slot an external caller never binds. FIX: a concrete per-call value never defers to an open-T (wildcard) entry — it dispatches concretely to fact Eq[Int]; an abstract per-call value (the enclosing T) still defers; a concrete call to a CONCRETE requires (Eq[T=Int]) still defers via the non-wildcard match. Also rewrote nth/member bodies ite(...)->if/then/else (ite is rule-only with no eval body, and as a strict fn would eagerly recurse), so nth is now EVALUABLE: nth([10,20,30],i) evals from an external namespace (eval_test wi414_nth_dispatches_concrete_eq). /code-review (soundness-focused): no regressions (concrete dispatch is MORE sound than deferring a concrete call to the enclosing-T requirement; a concrete type with no fact is a genuine error, not a defer). Full workspace suite green (1541). The CALL-SITE dual (member) is a DISTINCT mechanism → split to WI-415.", at: "2026-06-07T05:56:23Z")
